# Timeline — a sequence of things that happened, with a rail down the side and a
# dot on each entry. An audit log, a shipment's history, a release train, the
# activity on a record.
#
#     use Timeline():
#         use TimelineItem("09:14", "Request received"):
#             use Prose("From **ada** on the API key ending 9f2c.")
#         use TimelineItemCurrent("09:20", "In review"):
#             use Tag("Waiting on you", "warn")
#
# The body is a `slot`, not a `text` parameter, because what hangs off a timeline
# entry is never reliably one sentence — it is a paragraph, a tag, a link to the
# thing that changed, a small table, or nothing at all. The time and the title ARE
# parameters, because every entry has exactly one of each and a caller writing
# them into the slot would have to restate their type sizes.
#
# `TimelineItemCurrent` is the entry the reader is at: a filled dot instead of a
# ring. It is a second component rather than a `current: bool` for the reason
# `navigation/sidebaritem.fct` explains — a component has at most ONE `slot`, so
# the `if current: … / if !current: …` shape would need two of them and is
# refused. The alternative (one component, an invisible marker element, a `:has()`
# rule) is the right answer when the two states share a lot of markup; here the
# whole difference is two properties on a pseudo-element, so two components with
# one shared rule set is the smaller lie.
#
# ── WHERE THE RAIL STARTS AND STOPS ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The rail is drawn by the CONTAINER, not by the entries, and the reason is the
# same wrapper problem `data/keyvalue.fct` documents: a `use` renders an element
# around the component's nodes, so entries written as `use` calls are not DOM
# siblings and `:last-child` does not find the last one. A rail drawn per entry
# would therefore dangle below the final dot with no rule able to say "except this
# one". Drawn by the container it needs no such rule: it starts at the first dot
# (`top: 8px`) and stops `24px` short of the bottom — exactly the trailing padding
# every entry carries — so it ends level with the last entry's content and never
# hangs in space.
#
# A TIMELINE BUILT FROM A QUERY IS THE LOOP, as everywhere else in this library:
# `for e in Event by created desc class "x-timeline"` puts the rail on the region
# the runtime refills, and each `use TimelineItem(...)` inside it is an entry. A
# `for` dropped into this slot would be one entry containing the whole history.
app FacetData:
    css:
        [data-fa-mount] .x-timeline { position: relative; display: flex;
                       flex-direction: column; gap: 0;
                       border: none; background: none; padding: 0; }
        [data-fa-mount] .x-timeline::before { content: ""; position: absolute;
                       left: 5px; top: 8px; bottom: 24px; width: 2px; border-radius: 2px;
                       background: var(--x-line, #2f3336); }
        # `stretch`, not `flex-start`: in a COLUMN flex container `align-items` sizes
        # the children's WIDTH, and `flex-start` sizes each to its own content — which
        # leaves a `Code` block's `pre` no width to scroll inside, so a long line
        # sizes the whole box to max-content and the horizontal scrollbar lands on
        # the DOCUMENT. `marketing/steps.fct` carries the full note; the invariant is
        # that wide content scrolls inside its own container and the page body never
        # scrolls sideways. A child that must not be full width says `align-self`.
        [data-fa-mount] .x-tl-item { position: relative; display: flex;
                      flex-direction: column; align-items: stretch; gap: 4px;
                      padding: 0 0 24px 28px;
                      border: none; background: none; }
        # The dot sits on the rail rather than in a column of its own, so an entry
        # is one column of content and a long title wraps under itself instead of
        # under the dot. `box-sizing` is stated because the ring is a border and
        # the 12px is meant to be the outside of the dot.
        [data-fa-mount] .x-tl-item::before { content: ""; position: absolute; left: 0;
                      top: 5px; width: 12px; height: 12px; border-radius: 50%;
                      box-sizing: border-box; background: var(--fa-bg);
                      border: 2px solid var(--x-line, #2f3336); }
        [data-fa-mount] .x-tl-current::before { background: var(--fa-accent);
                        border-color: var(--fa-accent); }
        [data-fa-mount] .x-tl-when { display: block; color: var(--fa-muted);
                      font-size: 13px; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
        [data-fa-mount] .x-tl-title { display: block; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700;
                       line-height: 1.35; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
        # AN `if` THAT RENDERS NOTHING STILL LEAVES ITS ELEMENT BEHIND. A
        # conditional child is its own tracked region, so `if x != "":` with an
        # empty `x` produces an empty element — which costs nothing on its own and
        # costs one `gap` inside a flex container, appearing as unexplained air
        # under a stat with no delta or over a card with no title. An unclassed
        # empty element is always a region that rendered nothing, so hiding it is
        # always right; a classed one may be a deliberate empty box (a spinner's
        # ring, a marker element) and is left alone.
        [data-fa-mount] .x-tl-item > div:empty:not([class]) { display: none; }
    component Timeline():
        box class "x-timeline":
            slot
    component TimelineItem(when: text, title: text):
        box class "x-tl-item":
            if when != "":
                text "{when}" class "x-tl-when"
            text "{title}" class "x-tl-title"
            slot
    component TimelineItemCurrent(when: text, title: text):
        box class "x-tl-item x-tl-current":
            if when != "":
                text "{when}" class "x-tl-when"
            text "{title}" class "x-tl-title"
            slot
